# Optional. Used for absolute URLs in metadata (Open Graph, etc).
# Defaults to https://airrally.app if unset.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Required for authentication, profiles, favorites, and venue onboarding.
# Get these from your Supabase project: Settings -> API.
# Both key formats below are safe to expose to the browser — neither is a
# secret. Set whichever your project's dashboard shows you:
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co

# Current-generation key format (starts with sb_publishable_). Preferred
# when your project has one — checked first if both are set.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=your-publishable-key

# Legacy key format (a JWT). Still fully supported — used as a fallback
# when NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY isn't set.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key

# Server-only. NEVER prefix this with NEXT_PUBLIC_ and never import it from
# client code — see ARCHITECTURE.md. Not required for ordinary booking/
# payment flows, which still deliberately use narrowly-scoped SECURITY
# DEFINER Postgres functions instead (see ARCHITECTURE.md's Phase 4B
# section). Required ONLY for V1 rescheduling's complete_reschedule()/
# mark_reschedule_failed()/record_reschedule_refund_success() RPCs (see
# lib/supabase/serviceRole.ts and the reschedule production-readiness
# audit's finding B1) — those specific RPCs are restricted to the
# service_role Postgres role because no client-suppliable value can prove
# a payment/refund actually succeeded, unlike confirm_booking_payment()'s
# checkout-session-id check.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=your-secret-key

# Required for Phase 4B bookings/payments. Use TEST MODE keys
# (sk_test_.../whsec_...) during development — never live keys. Both are
# server-only: NEVER prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_, never import from a "use
# client" file. No publishable key is needed — Checkout Sessions are
# created server-side and the browser only redirects to the returned URL,
# it never loads Stripe.js.

# From your Stripe Dashboard's webhook endpoint settings (or `stripe
# listen`'s output if using the Stripe CLI locally).

# --- PayMongo (experimental, TEST MODE only — see ARCHITECTURE.md) -----
#
# A second, switchable payment provider behind the same seam as Stripe
# above. Both are server-only: NEVER prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_. Use TEST
# MODE keys (sk_test_...) only — never live keys.
PAYMONGO_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_your-test-mode-secret-key

# From a TEST MODE webhook endpoint you create in the PayMongo Dashboard
# (Developers > Webhooks) selecting the checkout_session.payment.paid
# event — the URL you point it at doesn't need to be reachable, this is
# only used to obtain a real signing secret for local signature
# verification (see scripts/verify-paymongo-checkout-flow.ts).
PAYMONGO_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_your-paymongo-webhook-signing-secret

# --- PayMongo Platforms marketplace split (see ARCHITECTURE.md) --------
#
# AIR/Rally's own PayMongo Platforms account id (org_...) — the
# recipient of the 5% platform_fee_amount on every split checkout
# session. There is no API to discover this automatically (the account
# used for this integration doesn't have permission to call
# /v1/organizations); find it in the PayMongo Dashboard under your
# account/organization settings and paste it here. Required only for a
# booking whose venue is a fully "activated" PayMongo Platforms child
# account — every other venue still uses the plain, non-split checkout
# and never needs this var.
PAYMONGO_PLATFORM_ACCOUNT_ID=org_your-platform-account-id

# --- Production safety kill switches (see lib/paymongoLaunchGates.ts) --
#
# The PayMongo marketplace split (95%/5%) and PayMongo refund execution
# are NOT production-ready yet (see the PayMongo Final Verification
# Report). Both default to disabled (unset, or anything other than the
# exact string "true"), so a mistaken env change can never accidentally
# activate unverified marketplace behavior. Do not set either to "true"
# until every item on that report's launch gate checklist is confirmed.
PAYMONGO_MARKETPLACE_SPLIT_ENABLED=false
# Opened deliberately (user decision, PayMongo-only migration): PayMongo
# is the sole provider, so a closed gate would mean no refund path exists
# at all. The two-party SPLIT refund remains unexercised in production;
# close this again by setting it to anything but "true".
PAYMONGO_REFUND_EXECUTION_ENABLED=true
# Adds PayMongo's processing fee to the customer's total instead of
# AIR/Rally absorbing it out of its 5% commission. OFF by default for one
# specific unresolved reason: confirm_paymongo_booking_payment()'s
# amount-integrity check reads the Payment object's amount, and whether
# that reports the original or grossed-up figure when this is on has not
# been observed against a real live payment. If it's the grossed-up
# figure, the check matches zero rows and no booking ever confirms. Enable
# in preview/test first, confirm one real booking reaches 'confirmed',
# only then set true in production. See lib/paymongoLaunchGates.ts.
PAYMONGO_PASS_ON_FEES_ENABLED=false

# --- PayMongo transfers (venue payouts) ---
# Sandbox only, and disabled by default. Enabling this does NOT make payouts
# work: AIR/Rally has no PayMongo wallet, so no source account exists to
# transfer from, and every provider method still refuses. The flag exists so
# sandbox work can begin the moment a wallet is provisioned.
# The adapter additionally refuses outright unless PAYMONGO_SECRET_KEY is a
# test key. See docs/payments/paymongo-transfers.md.
PAYMONGO_TRANSFERS_ENABLED=false

# --- Email notifications (see src/lib/services/email.ts) ---------------
#
# Every in-app notification is also emailed, via a Supabase Database
# Webhook (Database → Webhooks in the Supabase dashboard, NOT a migration
# — same posture as the PayMongo webhook URL) on INSERT to `notifications`,
# calling POST /api/webhooks/notification-created.
#
# From resend.com's dashboard, API Keys page. air-rally.com must be
# verified as a sending domain there first, or every send fails.
RESEND_API_KEY=re_your-resend-api-key
# Must be an address at the verified domain above, e.g. "AIR/Rally <notifications@air-rally.com>".
RESEND_FROM_EMAIL="AIR/Rally <notifications@air-rally.com>"
# A secret YOU generate (e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`) — set it here AND as
# the x-webhook-secret header value when configuring the Database Webhook.
# This is the entire authority on that route: it carries no user session
# and reaches the auth admin API, so an unset or guessable value here is a
# real hole, not a convenience default.
SUPABASE_DB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=generate-a-long-random-value

# Where a NON-PRODUCTION deployment's email goes.
#
# Every recipient is replaced by this one address and the intended recipient is
# carried in the subject, so staging can send real mail to a real inbox while
# nobody else can be reached. Leave UNSET on production -- it is ignored there.
#
# FAILS CLOSED: a deployment that is not production and has no value here sends
# NOTHING. That is deliberate. Staging holds accounts with real-looking
# addresses, and an unconfigured staging silently mailing them is worse than an
# unconfigured staging mailing no one.
EMAIL_REDIRECT_TO=
